            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.562,
            0.047,
            0.847,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Expressions Using Letter-Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "The expression means 5 times c is added to 3 times c is added to 10 times c. So in total, the letter-number c is added $( 5 + 3 + 1 0 )$ times. This is what we have seen as the distributive property of numbers. Thus,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.319,
            0.149,
            0.68,
            0.167
          ],
          "content": "5 \\times c + 3 \\times c + 10 \\times c = (5 + 3 + 10) \\times c",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.265,
            0.179,
            0.732,
            0.198
          ],
          "content": "(5 + 3 + 10) × c can be simplified to $1 8 \\times c = 1 8 c .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.212,
            0.766,
            0.236
          ],
          "content": "？ If $c = \\mp 5 0$ , find the total amount earned by the scale of pencils."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.254,
            0.846,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "？ Write the expression for the total money earned by selling erasers. Then, simplify the expression."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.304,
            0.848,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "The expression for the total money earned by selling pencils and erasers during these three days is 18c + 11d."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.35,
            0.663,
            0.373
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Can the expression 18c + 11d be simplified further?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.381,
            0.848,
            0.419
          ],
          "content": "There is no way of further simplifying this expression as it contains different letter-numbers. It is in its simplest form."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.419,
            0.848,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": "In this problem, we saw the expression 5c + 3c + 10c getting simplified to the expression 18c."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.465,
            0.848,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "？ Check that both expressions take the same value when c is replaced by different numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.525,
            0.848,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": "? **Example 6:** A big rectangle is split into two smaller rectangles as shown. Write an expression describing the area of the bigger rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.57,
            0.787,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.734,
            0.847,
            0.808
          ],
          "content": "The areas of the smaller rectangles are 4v sq. units and 3v sq. units. The area of the bigger rectangle can be found in two ways: (i) by directly using its side lengths v and (4 + 3), or (ii) by adding the areas of the smaller rectangles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.808,
            0.848,
            0.864
          ],
          "content": "The first way gives 7v, and the second way gives $4 \\nu   +   3 \\nu$ . We know that they are equal: $4v + 3v = 7v,$ and this is the required expression for the area of the bigger rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.864,
            0.848,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": "As earlier, a big rectangle is split into two smaller rectangles as shown below. Write an expression to find the area of the rectangle AEFD."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.812,
            0.939,
            0.84,
            0.954
          ],
          "content": "89"
        }
      ]
    }
